; 40-alias.kurt

; `alias NEW OLD` gives an existing symbol an additional name -- most often
; used to offer a nicer Unicode spelling next to an ASCII one.  `minimal.kurt`
; and the loaded theories already do this a lot, e.g. `implies` also answers
; to `⇒` (try `parse A ⇒ B` after declaring `bool A, B`) -- that's why lesson
; 20's proof-by-contradiction example could freely mix `implies`/`⇒` and
; `not`/`¬` if it wanted to.

infix "xor" 15 15
alias "^" xor          ; `^` is now just another name for `xor`

bool A, B
use A xor B
A ^ B                  ; same formula, either spelling

; An alias is resolved while scanning, before parsing even starts -- unlike
; `def` (11-def.kurt), which introduces a genuinely new constant and needs an
; inference step to unfold (see `doc/kurt-doc.md`'s 2025-04-17 notes).
